WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.015
Can a local propagation forecast help deliver an HF message before
its deadline?

00:00:05.015 --> 00:00:11.055
We tested five forwarding strategies. This globe replays decisions
made by the Linkwise simulator.

00:00:11.542 --> 00:00:20.031
We ran twenty matched seeds for each combination of network size,
traffic load, and fault. That produced twenty-seven hundred policy
runs.

00:00:20.031 --> 00:00:28.410
Every policy saw the same scheduled receiver windows and the same
underlying channel conditions. The calls it made determined its
own queues and collisions.

00:00:28.917 --> 00:00:36.407
Follow message six, from Spain to Norway. At fifteen oh six UTC,
earliest arrival calls the British relay.

00:00:36.407 --> 00:00:42.293
The relay accepts the payload. Its acknowledgment is lost, so the
sender cannot confirm the handoff.

00:00:42.792 --> 00:00:47.798
Switch to forecast plus history at the very same time. It skips
that opportunity.

00:00:47.798 --> 00:00:55.506
This example is the first message with different delivery outcomes
in seed seventeen. The full comparison includes every offered
message.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:03.971
At fifteen sixteen UTC, Spain calls France. This overlay uses the
previous minute’s NOAA ionospheric grid.

00:01:03.971 --> 00:01:15.729
At the path midpoint, vertical electron content is twenty-five
point four TEC units and the F two peak is two hundred sixty-seven
kilometers high. These readings provide context for the simulated
call.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:23.874
At fifteen forty, the British relay forwards the earliest-arrival
copy to Norway. It reaches the destination at the end of that
minute.

00:01:23.874 --> 00:01:30.754
On the other replay, the combined policy never completes the
route. Message six expires at fifteen fifty-five.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:39.549
These are real tmux panes showing the simulator output. On the
left, the final transfer follows earlier collisions and a lost
acknowledgment.

00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:46.823
On the right, the last attempted call fails. Both panes were
generated from the same scenario, traffic, and seed.

00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:55.353
Across twenty seeds, the combined policy reached seventy-two point
five percent priority-weighted delivery in the light European
workload.

00:01:55.353 --> 00:02:03.046
At commissioned load it fell to twenty-six point eight percent.
Earliest arrival reached thirty-two point one percent under those
same conditions.

00:02:03.542 --> 00:02:10.506
Could the six messages have fit? For this seed, an offline search
found a conflict-free schedule that delivered all six.

00:02:10.506 --> 00:02:20.084
The best online result was one of six. The offline scheduler knew
future channel outcomes; that advantage bounds what was physically
possible in this simulated world.

00:02:20.583 --> 00:02:29.113
At one thousand nodes every policy delivered zero under
commissioned load with open receiver windows. We tested an
alternative with preassigned callers.

00:02:29.113 --> 00:02:36.519
This globe follows an actual simulated delivery under those fixed
reservations. The large network uses synthetic stations.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:43.263
Earliest arrival delivered seven of twenty thousand offers with
assigned callers. Open windows delivered none.

00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:52.730
Just twenty-four of the nine hundred ninety-four access stations
had a grant in the four-hour schedule. Contention fell, but most
stations had no opportunity to send.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:03:03.139
In a separate experiment two real Linkwise modem processes
exchanged waveforms over a virtual cable. All five clean and all
five noise-only trials confirmed the payload.

00:03:03.139 --> 00:03:10.303
The two-path echo and the severe return-path fade each prevented
setup in all five trials. These are engineering stress cases.

00:03:10.792 --> 00:03:19.803
We also replayed over a thousand seconds of archived HF audio. Two
decoders agreed on eighteen valid receptions and sixteen distinct
frame hashes.

00:03:19.803 --> 00:03:29.856
Those recordings lack a transmitter log. The routing results are
simulation evidence. A field delivery claim still needs matched
two-way observations on held-out paths and dates.
